course: dictionary
lesson: 10256
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which meaning the writer means. The word tongue is a good example. «The honeybee drinks sweet nectar with its long tongue.» The bee is drinking, so tongue must mean the body part you use to taste and eat.
explain: Now read this: «The river ended in a long tongue of sand.» Sand cannot taste or speak, so tongue must mean a long, thin shape. Ask yourself: does this meaning fit the sentence? If it does not, try another meaning.
explain: Some tongue phrases are idioms, so the words mean something different from their usual meaning. «Bite your tongue» means stop yourself from saying something, and «tongue in cheek» means joking. You can only work these out from the context, or from a dictionary.
fact: Tongue has several meanings, so check the context before you choose one.
fact: «Tongue» means the soft, movable part inside your mouth that you use to taste, eat and speak.
fact: «Tongue» can also mean a language, as in «mother tongue», or the flap under the laces of a shoe.
fact: Your tongue is covered with taste buds that sense sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury tastes.
fact: «Hold your tongue» means stay quiet, and «on the tip of my tongue» means almost remembered.
exercise: «The snake flicked its forked tongue to smell the air.» What does tongue mean here? | A flap under the laces of a shoe | A language spoken by snakes | *The soft part inside the mouth used to sense smells | A long, thin patch of sand
exercise: «The old fisherman spoke his mother tongue at home.» What does tongue mean here? | The part of the mouth used for eating | *A language | A shape of fire | The flap under the laces
exercise: Ella says, «Please bite your tongue.» What does she mean? | *Stop yourself from saying something | Eat your food more slowly | Speak more loudly | Taste something sweet
test: «The river ended in a long tongue of sand.» What does tongue mean here? | *A long, thin shape like a tongue | The flap under the laces | A language | The part of the mouth used for speech
test: Which tastes can your tongue sense? | Crunchy and soft | Hot, cold and spicy | Only sweet | *Sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury
exam: Read this: «Ben said nothing and decided to hold his tongue.» What does hold his tongue mean? | *Stay quiet | Translate the book into French | Lick the bowl clean | Sing a song loudly
